WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
Once you have a type scale, the temptation
is to use size for every distinction.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:10.440
In interfaces, where space is tight, weight
and colour are often the better tools.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.340
You will limit yourself
to two or three weights,

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:19.140
check neutral colours against real backgrounds,
and stop using colour as the only signal.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.980
Most products need regular, 400, for body.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.560
Medium or semibold, 500 to
600, for labels and emphasis.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.900
Bold, 700, for a few headings.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.380
Loading five weights costs bandwidth
and invites inconsistency.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
If you use a variable font you
can pick any weight on its axis,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.400
but the same discipline applies:
name a few and use them.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.880
A short set of neutral text colours gives
you hierarchy without any change in size.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:57.160
On a white background, #737373
has a contrast ratio of

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.720
about 4.7 to 1, which passes
the WCAG AA requirement of 4.5

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:09.520
to 1 for normal text. Lighter greys often
look elegant in a mockup and fail in

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.900
practice. Check every text colour
against its real background,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.300
including hover and disabled states
that people still need to read.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.760
Before, a table used 14 pixel
bold for column headers,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:29.640
14 regular for cells, and 12
pixel light grey, #A3A3A3,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.680
for timestamps. The timestamps were
hard to read and failed contrast.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.960
Headers step back through colour
and gain clarity through weight.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.360
Timestamps are the same size as
the cells, so they stay readable,

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.220
but they are clearly secondary.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:51.080
Tabular numerals keep each digit the same
width, so columns of numbers line up.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.740
Proportional digits vary in width.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.040
Tabular figures make
numbers easy to compare.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.940
If the only difference between an error
and normal text is that it is red,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.400
some people will not see the difference.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.360
Pair colour with an icon,
a label such as “Error”,

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.820
or a change in weight.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.200
Small uppercase labels benefit
from a little extra tracking,

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:20.500
around 0.04em to 0.08em.
Large headings often

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:29.040
look better with slightly negative
tracking, around -0.01em to -0.02em.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.620
Body text should usually be left alone.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.540
Use two or three weights and
a few neutral text colours.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.000
Check every text colour for contrast
against its real background.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.280
Never let colour be the
only signal of meaning.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.020
The next course stays with that last
rule and takes it into components:

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:53.080
focus, the right element, and
labels people can actually use.
